\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The represented counting adjoint}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, #{V} a finite set and
#{H=\mathcal F_R(V)}. Equip #{H} with the
\vocabk{perfect counting duality}{fdip-001F}
#{b,\Phi,\Psi}, with #{H^\vee=\operatorname{Hom}_R(H,R)}.
For #{a\in\operatorname{End}_R(H)}, define
##{\begin{aligned}
a^\vee:H^\vee&\longrightarrow H^\vee,
&a^\vee(\ell)&=\ell\circ a,\\
a^\dagger&=\Psi\circ a^\vee\circ\Phi:H\longrightarrow H.
\end{aligned}}
The \vocabk{counting adjoint}{fdip-0007} #{a^\dagger} is the unique ordinary
#{R}-linear endomorphism satisfying
##{b(f,a^\dagger g)=b(af,g)\qquad(f,g\in H).}
It gives an \vocabk{ordinary #{R}-linear involution}{fdip-001G}
on the [composition algebra](fdip-0011)
#{\operatorname{End}_R(H)}. Products act with the right factor first.}

\p{The real incidence construction in \ref{fdip-0007} starts from
#{D:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^E} and returns an adjoint in the
opposite direction. Here #{a:H\to H} is an endomorphism over
the commutative ring #{R}. Both use the characterizing identity
that moves the operator between the two counting-pairing slots.
The existence and laws below follow from \ref{fdip-001F},
independently of the real incidence proof.}

\proof{
\p{The composite #{\ell\circ a} is linear, and precomposition is
linear in #{\ell}; thus all three arrows defining #{a^\dagger}
are linear. Since #{\Phi\Psi=I}, evaluation at #{f} gives the
displayed adjoint identity. Injectivity of #{\Phi} gives uniqueness
for each #{g}. In particular,
##{(a^\dagger g)(v)=b(a\delta_v,g).}
For #{a,c\in\operatorname{End}_R(H)} and #{r\in R}, bilinearity
and uniqueness give
##{(a+c)^\dagger=a^\dagger+c^\dagger,\quad
   (ra)^\dagger=ra^\dagger,\quad I^\dagger=I.}
Indeed the right-hand sides represent respectively
#{b((a+c)f,g)}, #{b((ra)f,g)} and #{b(f,g)}.}
\p{For products, applying the defining identity twice yields
##{b(f,c^\dagger a^\dagger g)
 =b(cf,a^\dagger g)=b(acf,g),}
so #{(ac)^\dagger=c^\dagger a^\dagger}. Symmetry of #{b} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
b(f,(a^\dagger)^\dagger g)
 &=b(a^\dagger f,g)=b(g,a^\dagger f)\\
 &=b(ag,f)=b(f,ag).
\end{aligned}}
Uniqueness therefore gives #{(a^\dagger)^\dagger=a}.}
}

\p{The construction also applies when #{V} is empty or #{R} is
the zero ring. Its representability is supplied by the explicit
inverse to #{\Phi}, not by a field hypothesis or a positivity
argument. Dual precomposition reverses composition before any
pairing is chosen; the Hom convention is
\citet{Section 10.10, Tag 0581}{stacks2017stacks}. The proof here
adds the specified symmetric counting identification with the dual.}
